{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed. A lottery is defined as right-skewed when a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (e.g., lower or zero payoffs) compared to favorable ones. For Lottery A: there is a 55% chance of 0 Euros (unfavorable) and 45% chance of 6000 Euros (favorable), so the unfavorable outcome has slightly higher probability. For Lottery B: 10% chance of 0 Euros (unfavorable) and 90% chance of 3000 Euros (favorable), so the favorable outcome dominates. Thus, Lottery A is more right-skewed than Lottery B (55% vs. 45% on unfavorable outcome vs. 10% vs. 90%). The reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more right-skewed. The information needed to apply this reason (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual first compared the outcomes and associated probabilities of the two lotteries. The key consideration mentioned is the higher chance of winning in Lottery B (90%). The individual explicitly states that they chose Lottery B because it is 'sicherer' (safer) with a 90% chance to win. This indicates a focus on the high probability of a positive outcome in B, and a preference for certainty or safety. There is no mention of skewness, distribution shape, or comparison of unfavorable versus favorable outcome probabilities beyond noting the high chance of winning in B. The justification centers on perceived safety due to high winning probability, not on the imbalance of probabilities toward unfavorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery has a greater concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes (i.e., more right-skewed) and preferring that one. The relevant information (e.g., 55% chance of 0 in A vs. 10% in B) is available, but the individual does not consider the higher probability of loss in A or frame the comparison in terms of skewness or concentration of unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual focuses on the high probability of winning in B as a positive, safe feature. The justification is aligned with risk aversion or preference for high winning likelihood, which is conceptually opposite to preferring more right-skewed (i.e., riskier in the downside) lotteries. There is no indication that the individual assessed skewness or preferred a lottery because it assigns more probability to bad outcomes. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
